# Cloudflare Deploy prompts for every active key in this file, so consumer
# deployment intentionally has none. The deploy wrapper creates Chickpea's
# stable auth authority and its one-time setup handoff automatically.

# Break-glass recovery is dormant by default. An account holder may create a
# temporary value only when recovery is needed:
# CHICKPEA_RECOVERY_TOKEN=""

# Legacy shared Admin login for existing installations during migration only.
# Fresh deployments must not enable it:
# TAG_ADMIN_TOKEN=""

# Non-secret defaults live in product code so Cloudflare does not expose them
# as customer-editable Deploy fields. Advanced operators may still set the
# documented runtime kill switches after deployment when needed.
# `CHICKPEA_LIVE_CHANNEL_CONFIG=false` restores write-once thread snapshots for
# new Channel admissions without changing already admitted TurnJobs.

# Channel memory is always on for eligible assigned channels. There is no
# memory feature flag or opt-out secret to configure.

# Optional: add a catalog provider after deploy. Without one, the seeded Default
# profile runs keyless on the Workers AI binding (@cf/zai-org/glm-5.2). These
# deliberately remain comments: the Deploy to Cloudflare button renders every
# active key in this file as a REQUIRED prompt, and provider keys must stay
# optional. Set any provider you want after deploy instead:
#   npx wrangler secret put ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
#   npx wrangler secret put OPENAI_API_KEY
#   npx wrangler secret put OPENROUTER_API_KEY
# Optional plain vars can label those environment credentials without exposing
# them and identify rotations: <PROVIDER>_CREDENTIAL_ALIAS and
# <PROVIDER>_CREDENTIAL_EPOCH. CHICKPEA_DEPLOYMENT_EPOCH identifies a keyless
# Workers AI binding deployment. Increment epochs when the underlying route is
# rotated; omit them to report "rotation unknown" honestly.
